# Navigator OS — Fedora Atomic image definition (Phase 1 draft)
#
# This Containerfile produces an immutable Navigator desktop image that can be
# built with bootc/rpm-ostree. It is based on Universal Blue's
# ublue-os/base-main base image (bootc-compatible, a monthly-updated Fedora
# Atomic derivative, a minimal base with no DE — we layer Hyprland on top).
#
# IMPORTANT: within Phase 1 this file is a DEFINITION ONLY.
# `podman build` / `rpm-ostree compose` MUST NOT BE RUN locally — the real
# build happens on GitHub Actions via .github/workflows/build-image.yml (see
# the project constraints: metered/roaming internet).

ARG BASE_IMAGE="ghcr.io/ublue-os/base-main"
# Fedora 44. It used to be pinned to 43: the fc44 chroot of the
# solopasha/hyprland COPR had not actually been REBUILT, the packages had been
# copied from fc43 ("forked" status in COPR), so aquamarine still wanted
# libdisplay-info.so.2 and would not depsolve against the .so.3 that F44 ships.
# Layer 1 now uses our own COPR (see below), so that obstacle is gone.
ARG BASE_TAG="44"

FROM ${BASE_IMAGE}:${BASE_TAG}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 1: base desktop packages + Hyprland (Wayland compositor)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hyprland, hypridle, hyprlock, hyprpaper and xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland are
# not in the official Fedora repositories. The solopasha/hyprland COPR used to
# be used; now denorath/navigator-hyprland is: 13 packages GENUINELY rebuilt
# against fedora-44 from the same spec repository
# (github.com/solopasha/hyprlandRPM).
#
# solopasha/hyprland is DELIBERATELY not enabled here — with both enabled, dnf
# picks the broken one. We have hyprland-0.51.1-1.fc44, while their fc44 chroot
# holds a copied 0.51.1-3.fc43, and because rpm compares the release first
# (3 > 1) theirs would win. The other packages Layer 1 needs (kitty, wofi,
# waybar, mate-polkit, seatd, NetworkManager, pipewire, wireplumber) are
# already in the official Fedora repository, so that COPR is not needed at all.
#
# hyprland 0.51.1 requires a single source patch on F44: WG21 P3953R3 renamed
# std::runtime_format to std::dynamic_format, and F44 ships libstdc++ 16 (F43
# had 15). The patch is on the navigator-f44 branch of the
# denorath-sys/hyprlandRPM repository; since upstream removed these calls after
# 0.51.1, it must be dropped on a version bump.
#
# mate-polkit is used instead of polkit-gnome (unmaintained upstream, removed
# from the Fedora repositories) — it is in the official Fedora repository, is
# GTK-based and does not depend on Qt6. The Hyprland-specific hyprpolkitagent
# was NOT TRIED: its hyprland-qt-support dependency has been pinned to Qt 6.9
# private API for months (verified in the build log) and conflicts with the
# Qt 6.11 that Layer 2's quickshell requires. This does not constrain us:
# hyprland itself does NOT REQUIRE hyprland-qtutils (verified in the repository
# metadata), so the hyprland-qt-support chain never enters the image.
#
# seatd: when trying to start Hyprland in a real VM in CI (see
# .github/workflows/build-disk-and-boot-test.yml, the hyprland-test job)
# Hyprland crashed with "CBackend::create() failed!". Verified in the
# aquamarine source: the DRM backend tries to open a seat with
# libseat_open_seat(); the base image only has libseat (the library), not seatd
# (the daemon) — `rpm -q seatd` returned "package seatd is not installed" and
# `loginctl` showed "-" in the SEAT column for real sessions (systemd-logind
# assigns no seat). seatd can manage device access itself even without a real
# VT/seat (e.g. this QEMU VM, and future remote/headless usage scenarios).
#
# hyprland-qtutils: while missing, Hyprland showed a yellow info banner on the
# desktop ("This is a runtime dependency for some dialogs") and the banner
# covered the right-hand side of the top panel (the assistant button + clock).
# Because it was not a config error, `hyprctl configerrors` was clean; it was
# never seen until the first screenshots were taken. The package does not exist
# in Fedora AT ALL (not even in dist-git), only in COPRs — so it was added to
# our own COPR together with its runtime dependency hyprland-qt-support (both
# built against fedora-44 from solopasha/hyprlandRPM specs; enabling
# solopasha's repository is not an option, see the "forked chroot" note above).
RUN dnf5 -y copr enable denorath/navigator-hyprland \
    && rpm-ostree install \
        hyprland \
        hyprland-qtutils \
        hyprpaper \
        hypridle \
        hyprlock \
        mate-polkit \
        xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland \
        kitty \
        wofi \
        waybar \
        NetworkManager \
        pipewire \
        wireplumber \
        seatd \
    && ostree container commit

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 2: Quickshell (Navigator's custom shell — see shell/README.md)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Quickshell is not in the official Fedora repositories; the
# errornointernet/quickshell COPR is the only actively maintained,
# automatically rebuilt source for Fedora 43/44. Unlike Layer 1's case, this
# COPR GENUINELY builds its fc44 chroot: the package is 0.3.0-3.fc44 and wants
# `Qt_6.11_PRIVATE_API`, while the qt6-qtbase in F44's official repository is
# 6.11.1 — so they match (verified in the repository metadata).
#
# The `dnf5 install` choice below dates from the F43 era: back then this build
# was compiled against the Qt 6.10 ABI, and because the base-main image carried
# an older layered qt6-qtbase (6.9.2) the depsolve conflicted.
# `rpm-ostree install` cannot upgrade a package in the base layer, and
# `rpm-ostree override replace` fails in this container-build flow with
# "Non-local replacement overrides not implemented yet" (even with
# --experimental). This layer therefore uses a plain `dnf5 install` — dnf5's
# normal dependency solver can upgrade the necessary packages without trouble,
# unlike rpm-ostree. On the F44 base this rationale appears to have
# disappeared: in the log of the first F44 build, qt6-qtbase-6.11.1 was
# installed directly from the `updates` repository WITHOUT an upgrade — meaning
# there is no old Qt in the base to conflict with. Even so, returning to
# `rpm-ostree install` was NOT TRIED: `dnf5 install` works and that should be a
# separate change. Two variables were not changed in the same build as the F44
# migration, so that if something broke it would be clear which one it was.
RUN dnf5 -y copr enable errornointernet/quickshell \
    && dnf5 -y install quickshell \
    && ostree container commit

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 3: Navigator brand/theme assets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONLY `palette.json` goes into the image from `theme/`, because it is the only
# asset that is currently real: `theme/gtk/` and `theme/qt/` are still empty
# skeletons (containing only a `.gitkeep`, see theme/README.md "Status").
# Layering empty directories would make a non-existent GTK/Qt theme look as
# though it were there — they will be added here once real assets exist.
#
# palette.json is the machine-readable single source of the brand colours: both
# `hyprland/hyprland.conf` (col.active_border, shadow) and `shell/Theme.qml`
# repeat the same hex values BY HAND (both note this in their own comments).
# That duplication is a drift risk; the consistency between the palette.json in
# the image and the hyprland.conf in the image is now genuinely verified in CI
# (see build-disk-and-boot-test.yml, the "Layer 3/4" step).
COPY theme/palette.json /usr/share/navigator/theme/palette.json

# Wallpaper: Navigator's brand image (1672x941). It was generated procedurally
# for a while (theme/generate-wallpaper.py); once the owner supplied the real
# brand image that generator was dropped — keeping two separate "wallpaper
# sources" would have been misleading.
#
# CI verifies that this GENUINELY appears on screen: the boot test compares the
# screenshot against this file block by block (see
# .github/scripts/analyze-screenshot.py). So the question answered is not "did
# hyprpaper run" but "is the user seeing this image".
COPY theme/wallpaper.png /usr/share/navigator/theme/wallpaper.png

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 4: Navigator system configs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hyprland.conf goes under /etc/skel as the system-wide default: useradd copies
# /etc/skel into the home directory when creating a new user, so every new
# Navigator user starts with this config but can afterwards freely modify their
# own ~/.config/hypr/hyprland.conf (image updates DO NOT OVERWRITE the user's
# file — /etc/skel is read only at account creation time).
#
# Before this layer, hyprland.conf did not exist in the real image at all; CI
# copied it from the runner to the VM with scp and tested that (a "CI
# simulation", as with ai-stack before Layer 5). The test now uses the image's
# own file.
COPY hyprland/hyprland.conf /etc/skel/.config/hypr/hyprland.conf

# The hyprpaper config lives under /etc/skel for the same reason: changing the
# wallpaper is the user's right and image updates must not overwrite their
# choice. hyprland.conf now contains `exec-once = hyprpaper`; a hyprpaper
# started without a config would give up in the first second, so the two went
# in together.
COPY hyprland/hyprpaper.conf /etc/skel/.config/hypr/hyprpaper.conf

# The credential configuration file — an EMPTY template (no secret enters the
# image, nor could it: the image is public).
#
# Why there is a template in the image: because /usr is read-only, cloud-bridge
# has to read the credential from the user's home directory
# (~/.config/navigator/env — see cloud_bridge/config.py). Rather than making
# the user guess the path of a file that does not exist, it is put in place,
# commented and documented, when the account is created.
#
# chmod 600 is mandatory and is done HERE: git does not store the file mode
# (apart from the exec bit), so COPY would leave 0644 — and cloud-bridge
# deliberately refuses a credential file that is readable beyond its owner.
# The file mode was measured to genuinely hold in the deployed image (the boot
# test, "ASSERTION: the template FILE must be closed to others").
#
# The directory's 0700, however, does NOT hold: in the same measurement
# /etc/skel/.config/navigator came out as 755 (run 30893404648). The chmod line
# still stands — the intent is right and it is effective at the container layer
# — but nothing relies on it: what protects the secret is the file's mode, and
# cloud-bridge looks at the file, not the directory. The directory mode is
# tracked in the boot test as a DIAGNOSTIC rather than an assertion; the
# mechanism (ostree's /etc merge, or loss at the layer) has not been pinned
# down yet.
COPY image/skel/.config/navigator/env /etc/skel/.config/navigator/env
RUN chmod 700 /etc/skel/.config/navigator \
    && chmod 600 /etc/skel/.config/navigator/env

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 5: Navigator AI stack (ai-stack/ — six Python modules)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ai-stack's six modules are copied under /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/ in a
# flat sibling hierarchy with EXACTLY the same directory names as in the
# repository. That hierarchy is not arbitrary but part of the runtime contract:
# the modules invoke each other as `python3 -m <module>` subprocesses using
# sibling paths relative to the cwd (router → ../local-runtime,
# ../cloud-bridge; local-runtime → ../hardware-probe; assistant → ../router,
# ../mcp-tools — see the defaults in the respective __main__.py files). If the
# directory names change, or the flatness is broken, the chain breaks silently;
# that is exactly what happened in a real CI attempt (hardware-probe was
# missing and router returned empty stdout).
#
# `shell/AssistantPanel.qml`'s `workingDirectory` also uses this path
# (/usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/router); until this layer was added, that path
# was simulated in CI with scp + `rpm-ostree usroverlay`. It is now present in
# the image itself, with /usr read-only.
#
# Only what is needed at runtime is copied: the package directory +
# pyproject.toml (metadata/entry point definition). `tests/` deliberately does
# not enter the image, and neither does `.env.local` (the real API key,
# gitignored) — the credential is never baked into the image. At runtime it
# comes from two sources: an environment variable
# (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY/ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN) or the user's own
# ~/.config/navigator/env file (whose template Layer 4 places under /etc/skel —
# see cloud_bridge/config.py).
#
# None of the six modules has any third-party dependency (all have
# `dependencies = []`, stdlib-only) — so this layer needs no pip or venv and
# installs no extra package.
COPY ai-stack/hardware-probe/hardware_probe/ /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/hardware-probe/hardware_probe/
COPY ai-stack/hardware-probe/pyproject.toml  /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/hardware-probe/
COPY ai-stack/local-runtime/local_runtime/   /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/local-runtime/local_runtime/
COPY ai-stack/local-runtime/pyproject.toml   /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/local-runtime/
COPY ai-stack/cloud-bridge/cloud_bridge/     /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/cloud-bridge/cloud_bridge/
COPY ai-stack/cloud-bridge/pyproject.toml    /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/cloud-bridge/
COPY ai-stack/mcp-tools/mcp_tools/           /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/mcp-tools/mcp_tools/
COPY ai-stack/mcp-tools/pyproject.toml       /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/mcp-tools/
COPY ai-stack/router/router/                 /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/router/router/
COPY ai-stack/router/pyproject.toml          /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/router/
COPY ai-stack/assistant/assistant/           /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/assistant/assistant/
COPY ai-stack/assistant/pyproject.toml       /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack/assistant/

# python3 already exists in the base-main image (verified in CI with
# `which python3`), but it is not left implicit, since it is ai-stack's only
# runtime dependency: the version is asserted here, at build time. That way, if
# the base image later drops python3 or falls below 3.11, the build breaks —
# rather than leaving a silently broken assistant at runtime.
#
# compileall: because /usr is read-only on a running system, Python cannot
# write __pycache__ at runtime and recompiles the source on every invocation
# (in this chain every request means 3-4 subprocesses). The bytecode is
# generated at build time. `checked-hash` is a deliberate choice: the ostree
# commit normalises all file mtimes, so the default timestamp-based
# invalidation would consider the .pyc files stale — hash-based validation is
# independent of mtime.
RUN python3 -c "import sys; assert sys.version_info >= (3, 11), f'ai-stack requires Python >= 3.11, found: {sys.version}'" \
    && python3 -m compileall -q --invalidation-mode checked-hash /usr/share/navigator/ai-stack \
    && ostree container commit

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 6: local model runtime (Ollama)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ollama is in the OFFICIAL Fedora 44 repository (0.12.11-4.fc44), so no COPR
# is involved here. That was checked rather than assumed, and it is the reason
# this layer is a plain `rpm-ostree install`.
#
# The package is well suited to an immutable image, which was also checked
# rather than assumed — it ships exactly three things that matter:
#   /usr/bin/ollama
#   /usr/lib/systemd/system/ollama.service
#   /usr/lib/sysusers.d/ollama.conf
# The sysusers file is the important one: the `ollama` user is declared rather
# than created by an RPM scriptlet, so systemd-sysusers creates it at boot.
# That is what an ostree deployment needs, since /var is empty until then.
#
# SIZE. The first version of this comment predicted "+345 MB compressed
# (+11%)" and was WRONG by a factor of seven. That prediction used Fedora's
# package-size, which is the compressed RPM *download*; an OCI layer carries
# the *installed* files, and rocblas is GPU kernel blobs that barely compress.
# The real, measured result of building this layer:
#
#   Navigator before: 3.26 GB compressed (281 layers)
#   Navigator after:  5.62 GB compressed (282 layers)
#   the new layer:    2364 MB — i.e. +72% on the whole image
#
# Kept as a lesson rather than quietly corrected: a size from package metadata
# is not a layer size, and this repository's own rule — measure, don't predict
# — applies to whoever is writing the rule down as much as to anyone else.
#
# The rocblas half is AMD GPU (ROCm) acceleration, arriving as a HARD
# dependency: ollama requires libhipblas.so.3, so it cannot be excluded
# without breaking the package. That looks like waste on a machine with no
# AMD card — but the alternatives were measured too, and they are worse:
# upstream's own tarballs for this release are 1047 MB (rocm) and 1421 MB
# (the default, which now bundles CUDA). The Fedora RPM is the SMALLEST
# option available, not merely the most convenient one.
#
# MODEL WEIGHTS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT SHIPPED. A model is another ~2 GB, and
# which model belongs on a machine is the user's decision, not the image's —
# `local-runtime` already maps a hardware tier to a recommendation
# (llama3.2:3b on a "low" tier, see local-runtime/models.py). Until the user
# runs `ollama pull`, the reported state is `model_ready: false` and the
# router falls through to the cloud, which is a path that has been verified
# in CI since long before this layer existed.
#
# The service is enabled so the desktop works without the user first learning
# that a daemon exists. Idle Ollama with no model loaded costs very little;
# it loads weights on demand.
RUN rpm-ostree install ollama \
    && systemctl enable ollama.service \
    && ostree container commit

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 7: Navigator shell (Quickshell QML — see shell/README.md)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 2 installs Quickshell ITSELF (the runner); this layer places
# Navigator's own QML shell. To run it:
#   qs -p /usr/share/navigator/shell/shell.qml
#
# Why /usr/share/navigator/shell/ (and not Quickshell's customary
# ~/.config/quickshell/ path): these are PROGRAM CODE, not user configuration.
# Had they been placed in /etc/skel, every user would get their own frozen copy
# and image updates could never update the shell (the exact opposite of what we
# want for hyprland.conf — there NOT OVERWRITING the user's file was right,
# here being able to update is right). A user who wants to run their own shell
# can copy this directory and point `qs -p` at their own path.
#
# The layer numbering follows the order in which the layers became REAL; it is
# not a dependency order. Placing it last is also deliberate: shell/ is the
# most frequently changing COPY content, and having it last preserves the build
# cache of the layers above it.
#
# Only the .qml files go in; shell/README.md is developer documentation and has
# no business in the image.
COPY shell/*.qml /usr/share/navigator/shell/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Metadata
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="Navigator OS"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.description="AI-native Linux desktop distribution — based on Fedora Atomic + Hyprland"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.vendor="Navigator OS Project"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.licenses="GPL-3.0-only"

# The standard label required for bootc compatibility
LABEL containers.bootc="1"
